// An all-fields literal must give every declared field a value (E0009). Where the literal names its
// type, the checker decides that: the type's field list, which of them declare a default, and which
// a `...base` spread contributes are all known before the program runs, so an incomplete literal is
// a compile error rather than a construction that gets as far as building the object.
//
// Both literals sit in functions nothing calls, so the rejection can only be the checker's. A
// program that reached its runtime would print `alive` and exit 0, and the two expectations below
// would fail — which is what distinguishes this from the same code raised at the same span by the
// construction-time check (`literal_missing_field_dynamic.noe`).
// expect: error E0009 at 24:12
// expect: error E0009 at 28:12
// expect: exit 1
struct Point {
    x: int
    y: int
    label: string = "origin"
}

struct Flat {
    x: int
}

fn omitted(): Point {
    return Point { x: 1 };
}

fn spread_too_narrow(): Point {
    return Point { ...Flat { x: 1 } };
}

echo "alive";
